      ":question/math-academy-id": "e-930",
      ":question/problem": "How much of a $2\\%$ acid solution must be added to $300$ milliliters of a $7\\%$ acid solution to get a solution that is $5\\%$ acid?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part} = \\textrm{percentage} \\times \\textrm{total}\n$$\n\nLet $x$ be the number of milliliters of the $2\\%$ acid solution that must be added to the $300$ milliliters of $7\\%$ acid solution. Then there will be a total of $300+x$ milliliters of the resulting $5\\%$ acid solution.\n\nWe organize this information in a percentage\u2013total\u2013part table:\n\n| % Acid | Total (ml) | Part Acid (ml) |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $7$ | $300$ | $0.07 \\cdot 300$ |\n| $2$ | $x$ | $0.02 \\cdot x$ |\n| $5$ | $300+x$ | $0.05 \\cdot (300+x)$ |\n\nThe parts acid in each of the $7\\%$ and $2\\%$ solutions add up to the part acid in the $5\\%$ solution. Therefore,\n\n$$\n0.07 \\cdot 300 + 0.02 \\cdot x = 0.05 \\cdot (300+x)\n$$\n\nSimplifying this equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.07 \\cdot 300 + 0.02 \\cdot x &= 0.05 \\cdot (300 + x) \\\\\n21 + 0.02x &= 15 + 0.05x \\\\\n6 + 0.02x &= 0.05x \\\\\n6 &= 0.03x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $100$ allows us to solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n100 \\cdot 6 &= 100 \\cdot 0.03x \\\\\n600 &= 3x \\\\\n200 &= x \\\\\nx &= 200\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $200$ milliliters of the $2\\%$ solution must be added."
    },
